    <string name="speedometer_settings_accuracy_title">How accurate is this?</string>
    <string name="speedometer_settings_accuracy_body">Speed comes from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals — a direct measurement of how fast you are moving, usually accurate to a fraction of a km/h. It is not worked out from how far you have travelled, which would be far noisier.\n\nThe signal indicator reflects what the receiver itself reports about its accuracy. When it says the signal is poor, treat the numbers as approximate.</string>

    <string name="speedometer_settings_dashboard_title">Why this disagrees with your car</string>
    <!--
      The single most useful thing this screen can say. Without it, a 6 km/h discrepancy reads as the
      app being broken, when in fact the dashboard is the less accurate of the two by design.
    -->
    <string name="speedometer_settings_dashboard_body">A car\'s speedometer is required by regulation never to read low, so manufacturers deliberately set it high — typically by 5 to 10 percent. A dashboard showing 100 km/h usually means about 92 to 95. Satellite speed has no such bias, so if the two disagree and this one reads lower, that is expected.\n\nThis does not make it a legal reference. Use the dashboard for that.</string>

    <string name="speedometer_settings_altitude_title">Altitude and sea level</string>
    <string name="speedometer_settings_altitude_body">Satellites report height above a smooth mathematical model of the Earth, not above sea level. The two differ by anywhere from −107 to +85 metres depending where you are. This app corrects for that using a bundled model of the Earth\'s gravity field, so the altitude shown is a true height above mean sea level.\n\nHeight is inherently the weakest part of a satellite fix — expect it to be several times less accurate than your position.</string>
</resources>
